      "title": "At Least 48 Civilians Killed in Weekend Attacks in Ethiopia's Oromia Region, Once Again",
      "link": "https://allafrica.com/stories/202610260665.html",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:09:23 GMT",
      "image": "https://cdn.allafrica.com/download/pic/main/main/csiid/00590863:5326aeafff94b3f910e610e0eecd8502:arc614x376:w1200.jpg",
      "content": "Ethiopia: At Least 48 Civilians Killed in Weekend Attacks in Ethiopia's Oromia Region, Once Again - allAfrica.com AllAfrica English (current) En Français Toggle navigation My Account Toggle navigation AllAfrica My Account English (current) En Français Ethiopia: At Least 48 Civilians Killed in Weekend Attacks in Ethiopia's Oromia Region, Once Again Peter Fitzgerald Ethiopia's regions, with Tigray and Amhara in the north, and Oromia further south. 26 October 2026 Addis Standard (Addis Ababa) Addis Abeba — At least 48 people have been killed and more than 31 others wounded, some severely, in attacks by armed men in Chole and Birbo kebeles of Shirka Woreda, Arsi Zone, in southeastern Oromia, residents who attended the victims' mass funeral told Addis Standard , describing door-to-door killings, looting and the use of heavy weapons. The attacks began on Saturday, 24 October evening, and continued into Sunday morning, 25 October, according to the residents. They said the victims included women, elderly people and at least six children under the age of 15, with many of those killed reportedly followers of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church. \"Armed men were going door to door shooting and stabbing civilians,\" one resident said, describing how local residents who had organized nighttime neighborhood watches were overwhelmed by heavily armed attackers carrying AK-47 rifles and other weapons. Keep up with the latest headlines on WhatsApp | LinkedIn The resident said the attacks lasted nearly six hours, leaving communities in the two localities devastated and many survivors too frightened to leave their homes. The second resident said rumors had circulated in nearby towns, including Gobesa, Sire and Kula, two days before the attacks, alleging that \"Tsimdo members\", were using churches to organize themselves. \"No one tries to ask questions. Everyone is passing on information like wildfire,\" the resident said, adding that the allegations had heightened fear in an area that, according to the account, had not previously experienced comparable violence, even during earlier attacks against Orthodox Christians in nearby localities of Shirka Woreda. The resident also said that local police supported the militias and said the attackers withdrew only after the federal army deployed to the area on Sunday evening. Addis Standard could not independently verify the allegations concerning the rumors, the attackers' affiliations or the role of local security forces. The resident said some people in Sire had prepared a letter on Friday to submit to the town administration, expressing fears about security and requesting protection, but their appeal was \"not received in time\". \"No one is interested to take us seriously,\" the resident said. Mass burial as residents remain in hiding The first 35 to 37 victims were buried in a mass grave on Sunday, according to the two residents. They said only a small number of people, \"overcoming their fear\", came out to participate in the burial, while many others remained in hiding. The death toll had reached 48 by the time the residents spoke to Addis Standard earlier today, they said, adding that additional burials were still underway and that some of the wounded were in critical condition. The injured were receiving treatment at hospitals in Gobesa and Shirka, while some had been referred to facilities as far away as Chanco, according to the accounts. The residents also reported the looting of a church in Digelu town and the use of heavy weapons during the attacks. Regional government blames 'Tsimdo' The Oromia Regional Government had not issued a formal statement specifically detailing the weekend attacks at the time of publication. However, regional Communication Bureau head Hailu Adugna gave a brief remark to state media about the loss of life and property damage and attributing the violence to \"Tsimdo.\" Adugna claimed \"Tsimdo, facing mounting losses, was attempting to divert the government's attention and expand internal conflict.\" Hailu did not provide details of the killings, including the number of victims, the circumstances of the attacks or the response by security forces. The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church had not issued an official statement on the latest attacks at the time of publication. Several local reports have referred to a newly formed militia structure in the region which is said to operate alongside existing regional security formations. Addis Standard could not independently verify the structure, mandate or chain of command of the reported formation. Attempts to reach the regional government's peace and security and police offices for comment were unsuccessful. Part of a wider pattern of killing in Oromia's Arsi The latest killings add up to years of attacks against civilians, particularly Orthodox Christian communities , in Arsi Zone of Oromia. Previous attacks have resulted in dozens of deaths, displacement and the destruction or looting of religious properties. Between 30 May and 1 June 2026, armed attacks in remote farming communities around Teleta Gebriel in Aseko Woreda killed civilians, displaced hundreds of residents into surrounding forests and resulted in the burning of the historic Teleta Saint Gabriel Church and the looting of Kara Kuftena Medhane Alem Church, according to previous reporting by Addis Standard . On 15 June, Abayneh Tebeqe, a priest serving at Gado Saint Mary Church in Shirka Woreda, was kidnapped from his farm in Mitana Gado and subsequently killed, according to reports published at the time. Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Top Headlines Ethiopia Legal Affairs Conflict Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later. Earlier attacks have also targeted civilians in the area. On 13 August 2024, an attack in Tijo village, Aseko Woreda, left six civilians dead, including a local priest, and three others injured , according to previous Addis Standard reporting. In November and December 2023, separate attacks in Shirka and surrounding areas reportedly killed more than three dozen people, with Orthodox Christian families and worshippers among those targeted. In July 2026, the Ethiopian Human Rights Council reported that at least 432 civilians and four local militia members had been killed in targeted attacks across East Arsi since 2021/2022. The rights group documented killings, kidnappings for ransom and forced displacement, with Orthodox Christian communities disproportionately affected, according to its report. The repeated attacks have prompted condemnations from religious leaders and councils, including calls for independent investigations, accountability and measures to protect civilians. Responsibility for these killings has remained elusive, with government officials previously attributing some attacks to the armed group they refer to as \"OLF/Shene\". Read the original article on Addis Standard . Tagged: Ethiopia East Africa Legal Affairs Conflict Human Rights Copyright © 2026 Addis Standard . All rights reserved. Distributed by AllAfrica Global Media ( allAfrica.com ). To contact the copyright holder directly for corrections — or for permission to republish or make other authorized use of this material, click here. AllAfrica publishes around 600 reports a day from more than 90 news organizations and over 500 other institutions and individuals , representing a diversity of positions on every topic. We publish news and views ranging from vigorous opponents of governments to government publications and spokespersons. Publishers named above each report are responsible for their own content, which AllAfrica does not have the legal right to edit or correct. Articles and commentaries that identify allAfrica.com as the publisher are produced or commissioned by AllAfrica . To address comments or complaints, please Contact us . AllAfrica is a voice of, by and about Africa - aggregating, producing and distributing 600 news and information items daily from over 90 African news organizations and our own reporters to an African and global public. We operate from Cape Town, Dakar, Abuja, Johannesburg, Nairobi and Washington DC. Support our work Sign up for our newsletter For Advertisers Subscribe © 2026 AllAfrica Privacy Policy Contact Us About Us AllAfrica - All the Time Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later.",
      "summary": "At least 48 civilians were killed and over 31 injured in a series of attacks by armed men in the Oromia region of Ethiopia over the weekend. The violence occurred in Chole and Birbo kebeles of Shirka Woreda, where residents reported door-to-door killings, looting, and the use of heavy weaponry. The attacks began on the evening of October 24 and continued into the following morning, targeting primarily followers of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, including women and children. Local residents described being overwhelmed by heavily armed attackers, and many survivors remain too frightened to leave their homes. The Oromia Regional Government attributed the violence to a group referred to as \"Tsimdo,\" although details regarding the attackers and the response from security forces remain unclear. This incident is part of a troubling pattern of violence against civilians in the region, particularly targeting Orthodox Christian communities, with previous attacks resulting in significant casualties and destruction.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/at-least-48-civilians-killed-in-weekend-attacks-in-ethiopia--2375cea859.html"
