o we are » About us Leadership Strategic planning and results Multi-Year Appeal What we do » Prevention and mediation Peacebuilding Electoral assistance Gender, women, peace and security Climate, peace and security Innovation Support to Member-State bodies Regional organizations Where we work » Our global presence Special political missions Liaison presences Peace and Development Advisors Latest » News News from our missions Speeches and statements Press Releases Politically Speaking Resources » Document Library Home Who we are About us Leadership Strategic planning and results Multi-Year Appeal What we do Prevention and mediation Peacebuilding Electoral assistance Gender, women, peace and security Climate, peace and security Innovation Support to Member-State bodies Regional organizations Where we work Our global presence Special political missions Liaison presences Peace and Development Advisors Latest News News from our missions Speeches and statements Press Releases Politically Speaking Resources Document Library DPPA Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs Home News Today's top news: Somalia, Iraq/Kuwait Today's top news: Somalia, Iraq/Kuwait 26 October 2026 Today's top news Somalia A formal ceremony was held in Mogadishu, marking the end of the mandate of the UN Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS) , led by the Officer-in-Charge of the Mission, Raisedon Zenenga, and the Deputy Prime Minister of the Federal Government of Somalia, Salah Jama. Statement attributable to the Spokesperson for the Secretary-General: On 31 October, the UN Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS) will complete its two-year transition and cease operations, pursuant to Security Council resolutions 2753 (2024) and 2809 (2025) and in line with the request of the Federal Government of Somalia. The mandated functions of the Mission have been transferred to Somali institutions and UN agencies, funds and programmes, reflecting Somalia's commitment to great national ownership of its peacebuilding and state-building agenda. The Secretary-General commends the progress achieved over the past decades towards building peace and security in Somalia. He commends the determination and resilience of the Somali people despite the many challenges they face and acknowledges the tremendous efforts made on the country’s journey from conflict to recovery. The UN will continue to support Somalia through its resident agencies, funds and programmes, working in partnership with Somali authorities, the African Union and other international partners to advance peace, stability, human rights and sustainable development. The Secretary-General reaffirms the enduring commitment of the United Nations to the people of Somalia and to supporting Somali-led efforts to build a peaceful, stable and prosperous future. His full statement has just been shared with you now and it is also available online. Read the full statement by the Secretary-General here . Iraq/Kuwait On 26 October, Mari Yamashita, Senior Representative of the Secretary-General for the implementation of Security Council resolution 2792 (2025), briefed the Security Council on efforts to clarify the fate of missing Kuwaiti and third-country nationals and recover missing Kuwaiti property, including national archives. She noted that 315 people remain missing more than 35 years after Iraq’s invasion of Kuwait, stressing the urgency of identifying witnesses and pursuing credible leads. Yamashita called for stronger cooperation between Iraq, Kuwait and the International Committee of the Red Cross, emphasizing the humanitarian imperative of providing answers to families and advancing the return of missing property. Read her full remarks to the Security Council here . Share Today's top news Related News This Week in DPPA: 17 - 23 October 2026 This Week in DPPA: 17 - 23 October 2026 A brief roundup of United Nations political and peacebuilding events and developments globally. Ruiz Massieu calls for coordinated action to advance Haiti’s political transition and security On 19 October, Special… African Union Peace and Security Council and UN Peacebuilding Commission strengthen cooperation on peacebuilding in Africa African Union Peace and Security Council and UN Peacebuilding Commission strengthen cooperation on peacebuilding in Africa On 21 October 2026, the African Union Peace and Security Council (AU PSC) and the United Nations Peacebuilding Commission (PBC) held their Ninth Annual Consultative Meeting at UN Headquarters in New York. The meeting, held… Today's top news: Colombia, Libya Today's top news: Colombia, Libya Colombia In Timbío, Cauca, the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia (UNVMC) , together with the Entrepreneurship Unit of the Mayor’s Office, visited productive initiatives led by peace agreement signatories and… Sudan war: Peace efforts resume to end ‘drone war’ fuelling humanitarian crisis Sudan war: Peace efforts resume to end ‘drone war’ fuelling humanitarian crisis Talks to halt the fighting in Sudan and alleviate the humanitarian crisis there continued in Geneva on Wednesday, where nearly two dozen countries convened to discuss a pathway to a ceasefire and transition to peaceful… Department of Political and Peacebuilding Affairs DPPA is at the heart of United Nations efforts to prevent and resolve conflict, advance diplomacy and mediation, and build sustainable peace around the world. A-Z Site Index Contact Copyright FAQ Fraud Alert Privacy Notice Terms of Use Donate Go to top",
      "summary": "On October 26, 2026, a formal ceremony in Mogadishu marked the conclusion of the UN Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS), which will officially cease operations on October 31. This transition, requested by the Federal Government of Somalia, reflects a significant shift towards national ownership of peacebuilding efforts. The UN Secretary-General praised the progress made in Somalia over the past decades and reaffirmed the UN's commitment to supporting the country through various agencies and partnerships aimed at promoting peace, stability, and sustainable development.\n\nIn a separate briefing to the Security Council, Mari Yamashita highlighted ongoing efforts to address the fate of 315 missing individuals and the recovery of Kuwaiti property, including national archives, following Iraq’s invasion of Kuwait over 35 years ago. She emphasized the need for enhanced cooperation among Iraq, Kuwait, and the International Committee of the Red Cross to provide answers to affected families and facilitate the return of missing property.",
