      "title": "Battle on the Road to Burhakaba: Somalia’s Federal Government Forces Suffer Heavy Losses - Garowe Online",
      "link": "https://www.garoweonline.com/en/news/somalia/battle-on-the-road-to-burhakaba-somalia-s-federal-government-forces-suffer-heavy-losses",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:30:41 GMT",
      "image": "https://uploads-garowe.s3.amazonaws.com/upload/images/Article/2026/1793050132/main/og.jpeg",
      "content": "Battle on the Road to Burhakaba: Somalia’s Federal Government Forces Suffer Heavy Losses Latest Headlines Hassan Sheikh Reportedly Fails to Secure Museveni’s Commitment to Keep Ugandan Troops in Somalia Hassan Sheikh Reportedly Fails to Secure Museveni’s Commitment to Keep Ugandan Troops in Somalia Battle on the Road to Burhakaba: Somalia’s Federal Government Forces Suffer Heavy Losses Somalia’s Election Crisis: What Lies Behind Hassan Sheikh’s Uganda Trip? Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute Advertise with us Home News Puntland Somalia World Sports Press Releases Somaliland Videos Opinion Contact Privacy Policy English Somali Battle on the Road to Burhakaba: Somalia’s Federal Government Forces Suffer Heavy Losses GAROWE ONLINE Posted On 26-10-2026, 09:25PM MOGADISHU— Forces loyal to Somalia’s federal government, whose term in office has expired, reportedly suffered heavy casualties and lost military vehicles during fierce fighting between Leego and Jameecada as they advanced toward Buurhakaba in the Bay region. The troops, comprising units from the elite Gorgor and Danab forces, had been deployed to recapture Buurhakaba, approximately 60 kilometres from Baidoa. The town is currently controlled by forces loyal to exiled South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed, widely known as Laftagareen. The military operation is linked to Villa Somalia’s efforts to consolidate its authority over South West State through a rival administration established in Baidoa, headed by former Speaker of the Federal Parliament Aden Mohamed Nur, commonly known as Aden Madobe, whose term in office has also expired. The legitimacy of the process through which the administration was established remains disputed. Madobe, who has been staying in Mogadishu in recent days, reportedly made his return to Baidoa conditional on federal forces first retaking Buurhakaba. Meanwhile, forces loyal to Laftagareen have captured several areas across the Bay and Bakool regions. Security sources said military officers commanding the federal troops had warned against advancing along the Mogadishu–Baidoa highway, a route that has remained vulnerable to Al-Shabaab attacks for years. Their concerns were reportedly dismissed, and the operation was ordered to proceed. The troops encountered heavy rainfall, difficult road conditions and multiple ambushes before coming under intense attack near Jameecada. Reports indicate that numerous soldiers were killed and 36 military vehicles were lost, including six Turkish-made Kirpi armoured vehicles. Officers reported missing include Mahad Caamir, Anas Dheere, Jeego, Abdikarim and Ahmed Gaashaan. Halac, commander of the 64th Battalion of the 7th Brigade, was reportedly wounded. Pro-Al-Shabaab online platforms circulated images and videos purporting to show military vehicles captured from federal forces, alongside others destroyed during the fighting. Separate reports also alleged that Turkish military aircraft mistakenly struck Somali government troops, potentially contributing to the losses. The latest clashes followed federal military operations along the Mogadishu–Baidoa highway, including fighting around Leego on October 24. The area has witnessed major confrontations in the past, notably Al-Shabaab’s deadly 2015 assault on Burundian troops serving with the African Union mission. The fighting also comes amid growing regional concerns over Somalia’s political crisis. Uganda has reportedly warned that it could withdraw its troops from Somalia unless the country’s deepening political disputes are resolved. President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud travelled to Uganda on October 26, shortly after announcing plans for a national consultative meeting with opposition leaders and regional administrations. The central question is why Villa Somalia has chosen this moment to risk troops and military equipment along a highway long plagued by insecurity, particularly when the operation appears closely tied to efforts to strengthen Aden Madobe’s administration rather than prioritising the security of the strategic Mogadishu–Baidoa corridor. Latest Headlines Hassan Sheikh Reportedly Fails to Secure Museveni’s Commitment to Keep Ugandan Troops in Somalia 26-10-2026, 10:23PM Hassan Sheikh Reportedly Fails to Secure Museveni’s Commitment to Keep Ugandan Troops in Somalia 26-10-2026, 10:23PM Somalia’s Election Crisis: What Lies Behind Hassan Sheikh’s Uganda Trip? 26-10-2026, 12:13PM Puntland President Warns Somalia Faces Threat to Statehood Amid Election Dispute 25-10-2026, 12:29PM Advertise with us Advertise with GaroweOnline and expose your brand to a global audience of loyal visitors from across the world $50 $25 $10 $5 Other $ ONE TIME MONTHLY DONATE GET IN TOUCH Latest Videos Jubaland president Ahmed Madobe sets conditions on election Videos 29-11-2020 01:12AM Federal Interior minister speaks about next eelctions Videos 16-11-2020 01:31PM Somaliland suspends engagement with UN humanitarian agencies Videos 01-11-2020 05:26PM Child traffickers arrested in Somalia Videos 23-10-2020 10:52PM Related Articles Hormuud Telecom hosts fifth Somali Success Story Forum focused on healthcare sector He also conveyed congratulations from Senate Speaker Abdi Hashi Abdullahi to Somalia’s Success Story Forum (BGS26). Somalia 21-10-2026 09:30AM Somalia: Puntland Health Minister Inspects Dhahar Hospital During Regional Tour He later visited Dhahar District Hospital and several other health facilities to assess their operations and the medical services available to residents. Somalia 21-10-2026 04:40AM Somalia’s Opposition Alliance to Hold Key Political Talks in Nairobi, Kenya Somalia 19-10-2026 09:27AM Fahad Yasin Accuses Hassan Sheikh of Leading Somalia Toward Two Governments and Two Armies Somalia 16-10-2026 10:23PM Somalia : Hassan Sheikh Visits Turkish Vessel in Move Seen as Public Diversion Somalia 16-10-2026 07:51PM Somalia: Laftagareen’s Forces Expand Territorial Gains in Southwest as Buurhakaba Falls Somalia 15-10-2026 05:39PM Home News Videos Gallery Opinion About Us Contact Privacy Policy Copyright © GAROWONLINE All Rights Reserved Advertise with us Advertise with GaroweOnline and expose your brand to a global audience of loyal visitors from across the world $50 $25 $10 $5 Other $ ONE TIME MONTHLY DONATE GET IN TOUCH Are you happy to accept cookies? To manage your cookie choices now, including how to opt out where our partners rely on legitimate interests to use your information. Yes, I’m happy",
      "summary": "Somalia's federal government forces have faced significant losses during a military operation aimed at recapturing Buurhakaba, a town currently held by forces loyal to exiled South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed, known as Laftagareen. The elite Gorgor and Danab units suffered heavy casualties and lost 36 military vehicles, including six Turkish-made Kirpi armored vehicles, during intense fighting along the Mogadishu-Baidoa highway. The operation, which has been criticized for its timing and execution, coincides with ongoing political instability in Somalia, as President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud seeks to consolidate authority amid disputes over the legitimacy of rival administrations. Concerns have also been raised about the vulnerability of federal troops to Al-Shabaab attacks, which have historically plagued the region. The situation is further complicated by Uganda's warning of potential troop withdrawal unless Somalia's political crisis is resolved, highlighting the precarious security landscape in the country.",
