      "title": "Somalia Baidoa Clashes: Federal and Regional Forces Fight - https://kulu-media.com/",
      "link": "https://kulu-media.com/somalia-baidoa-clashes-federal-regional-forces/",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:37:30 GMT",
      "image": "https://kulu-media.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/somalia-baidoa-clashes-security-patrol-1000x600.jpg",
      "content": "Somalia Baidoa Clashes: Federal and Regional Forces Fight | Kulu Media Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Connect with us Search Kulu Media Somali Federal Troops Clash With Regional Forces Near Baidoa Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Home » News » Africa » Horn of Africa » Somali Federal Troops Clash With Regional Forces Near Baidoa Horn of Africa Somali Federal Troops Clash With Regional Forces Near Baidoa Federal troops and Laftagareen loyalists clash outside Baidoa amid rival claims. Published 45 seconds ago on October 26, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Security personnel patrol a street in Baidoa, Somalia, on September 3, 2026. Renewed clashes were reported outside the city on October 26. (AP Photo/File) Somali federal troops clashed with fighters loyal to former South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen near Baidoa on October 26, 2026, as a continuing dispute over regional control triggered renewed fighting in southern Somalia. The Somalia Baidoa clashes erupted around Burjeedka, on the outskirts of the city, according to AFP reporting . Residents reported explosions, gunfire and military reinforcements moving toward the area. A Somali army commander, Abdullahi Hassan, said government forces had inflicted casualties on their opponents but acknowledged that two soldiers had been killed. A commander loyal to Laftagareen, Hassan Abdulkadir Mohamed, disputed the government’s account, claiming his fighters had forced federal troops to retreat toward Baidoa. Neither side’s claims of battlefield success could be independently verified, and the overall casualty toll remained unknown. Rival Forces Dispute Control of the Battlefield The latest fighting follows weeks of military preparations and territorial changes across South West State. Federal and regional authorities had previously announced plans to recover several towns captured by Laftagareen-aligned fighters. On October 18, Kulu Media documented counteroffensive preparations involving federal and South West State forces. Those preparations focused on strategic locations including Qansaxdheere, Berdale, Buurhakaba, Awdiinle and Diinsoor. The October 26 confrontation near Burjeedka appears to represent another phase of the military struggle surrounding Baidoa, although the precise relationship between the earlier preparations and the latest fighting has not been independently established. Both sides have offered competing accounts of their operations. Witness reports establish that fighting occurred near Baidoa, but the available information does not independently confirm which side controlled the contested positions afterward. Territorial Advances Increased Pressure on Baidoa The latest confrontation follows significant territorial advances by fighters loyal to Laftagareen earlier this month. Between October 8 and 17, his forces reportedly took control of several district centers in the Bay region. Kulu Media previously reported that Laftagareen’s forces seized Diinsoor , along with Berdale, Buurhakaba and Qansaxdheere. The territorial changes brought opposition-aligned fighters closer to Baidoa, the administrative center of South West State. Buurhakaba is particularly important because it lies along the main road connecting Mogadishu and Baidoa, a major transportation corridor for civilian supplies, commercial activity and military movements. Awdiinle, another strategically significant settlement, is located approximately 30 kilometers from Baidoa. The federal government-backed regional administration has rejected the territorial advances and pledged to recover the affected areas. However, the extent of either side’s control over surrounding rural areas remains uncertain. Political Dispute Fuels Repeated Armed Confrontations The conflict stems from a prolonged confrontation between Somalia’s federal government and Laftagareen over political authority in South West State. Federal forces entered Baidoa on March 30, 2026, forcing Laftagareen from the regional capital. Although he subsequently announced his resignation, his supporters continued challenging the legitimacy of the federal government-backed administration. The dispute has repeatedly developed into armed confrontations. On September 3, fighting erupted in Baidoa between federal government forces and militias aligned with Laftagareen. According to the Associated Press, Bay Regional Hospital received 71 wounded people and one dead civilian following those clashes. Somalia’s Defense Ministry accused Laftagareen-aligned fighters of participating in the attack and alleged involvement by Al-Shabaab. Laftagareen’s supporters rejected allegations that they were cooperating with the militant organization. The allegations have not been independently substantiated. The September confrontation also coincided with a reported Doolow drone strike targeting the residence of former Somali commander Mahad Abdirahman Aden, widely known as Mahad Shub. Mahad, a relative and political ally of Laftagareen, survived the attack. The incident generated competing allegations over responsibility, but no independently verified evidence conclusively established who carried out the strike. Together, these developments illustrate how the political confrontation has expanded into a wider security dispute involving military operations, territorial control and competing claims of legitimacy. Civilian Risks Grow Amid Renewed Fighting The renewed violence raises concerns for civilians in and around Baidoa, a major humanitarian center hosting large numbers of people displaced by conflict, drought and food insecurity. Repeated confrontations have disrupted daily life and threatened access to essential services. During the September clashes, residents reported heavy gunfire near populated areas, while medical facilities treated dozens of wounded people. Further fighting around Baidoa could threaten transportation routes used to deliver food, medicine and humanitarian supplies. The latest clashes occurred outside the city, but continued military operations could increase risks for surrounding communities. There was no independently confirmed information on civilian casualties from the October 26 fighting at the time of reporting. Wider Regional and Political Implications The confrontation also comes as Somalia continues its military campaign against Al-Shabaab while facing disagreements over federal authority, constitutional arrangements and elections. Renewed fighting between government forces and rival regional fighters could divert military resources from counterinsurgency operations. The crisis also has implications for neighboring Ethiopia, which maintains troops in Somalia under regional security arrangements. Earlier this month, Ethiopia and Somalia held diplomatic discussions concerning Somali military personnel held in Jigjiga. That separate dispute demonstrates the wider regional security sensitivities surrounding Somalia’s internal military and political developments, although no direct connection to the October 26 Baidoa clashes has been established. For now, the immediate outcome of the fighting near Burjeedka remains disputed. Both federal forces and Laftagareen-aligned fighters have claimed military success, while independent confirmation of territorial control and the full casualty toll remains unavailable. The renewed confrontation underscores the continuing instability in South West State, where political disagreements have developed into repeated armed clashes despite earlier efforts to consolidate federal authority. Related Topics: Baidoa Laftagareen Somali National Army Somalia South West State Don't Miss Senior TPLF Commander John Medid Captured by Ethiopian Forces, TPF Sources Say Continue Reading You may like Somalia Forces Prepare to Retake Towns Seized by Laftagareen Loyalists Laftagareen Forces Seize Diinsoor as Somalia Regional Crisis Deepens Somaliland Welcomes Trump’s Remarks, but US Recognition Remains Unconfirmed Ethiopia Over 14,000 Ethiopians Flee Moyale Fighting Into Kenya Over 14,000 flee Moyale clashes as humanitarian needs surge in Kenya. Published 2 days ago on October 25, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopians who fled fighting in Moyale gather at a holding area in Kenya on October 23, 2026. Photo: Kenya Red Cross Society More than 14,000 Ethiopians have fled deadly fighting in the southern border town of Moyale into neighboring Kenya, as humanitarian agencies warn that shortages of food, clean water and sanitation facilities are threatening thousands of displaced families. The latest figures, reported on Saturday, October 24, 2026, mark a sharp escalation in the Moyale fighting displacement crisis, which began on October 21 amid tensions involving communities from Ethiopia’s Oromia and Somali regional states. According to Anadolu Agency , Kenya Red Cross officials said more than 10,900 displaced people were sheltering in six temporary camps in the Kenyan border town of Moyale, while approximately 4,000 others were staying with relatives and host families. The reported numbers represent a substantial increase from the 2,500 displaced Ethiopians documented in earlier reports on October 23. The growing number of arrivals has intensified pressure on humanitarian services in the Kenyan border region, where emergency responders are struggling to meet the needs of families fleeing the violence. Kenya Red Cross Warns of Critical Shortages The Kenya Red Cross has appealed for additional humanitarian assistance as displaced families face increasingly difficult living conditions. Malik Adan, the organization’s head of the Upper Eastern region, told reporters that emergency shelters urgently needed more food, drinking water, sanitation supplies and toilet facilities. Humanitarian officials warned that inadequate sanitation could increase the risk of diarrheal diseases, particularly in crowded temporary camps where access to clean water and hygiene facilities is limited. The Kenya Red Cross has distributed tents, blankets, sleeping mats and other essential supplies while providing nutritional assistance to children and breastfeeding mothers. Local authorities have also pledged additional food supplies, including rice and beans, but relief workers remain concerned that available resources may be insufficient for the growing displaced population. The latest displacement figures underscore the rapid deterioration of humanitarian conditions since fighting erupted earlier in the week. When the violence first intensified, Kenyan authorities reported that hundreds of Ethiopians had crossed the border, many seeking temporary shelter with relatives and local residents. The movement has since expanded into a much larger humanitarian emergency. Death Toll Feared to Rise as Fighting Continues The humanitarian crisis follows several days of deadly clashes on the Ethiopian side of Moyale. At least nine deaths were reported on October 23, according to hospital information cited in earlier reporting. Humanitarian officials now fear that the overall death toll may have exceeded a dozen, although an updated casualty figure has not been independently confirmed. Residents have reported continued gunfire and shelling in affected areas, raising concerns about further casualties and displacement. The fighting has been linked to tensions between Oromo and Somali communities in southern Ethiopia. According to Anadolu Agency, a dispute involving the display of the Somali Regional State flag in an area administered by Oromia contributed to the latest confrontation. However, the precise sequence of events that triggered the violence, the identities of all armed participants and responsibility for individual attacks remain incompletely verified. The fighting has also affected civilians on the Kenyan side of the border. Earlier reports documented a woman injured by a stray bullet in Kenyan Moyale, demonstrating how violence close to the international boundary can threaten communities in both countries. Cross-Border Trade and Local Communities Under Pressure Moyale is one of the most important border crossings connecting Ethiopia and Kenya. The town serves as a commercial and transportation link between the two countries, supporting cross-border trade and economic activity. Continued insecurity threatens to disrupt business operations, transportation and the movement of goods, potentially affecting communities whose livelihoods depend on the border economy. The arrival of thousands of displaced Ethiopians has also placed additional pressure on Kenyan host communities. While temporary camps accommodate most of those reported to have crossed the border, thousands are staying with relatives and local families. Such arrangements can provide immediate protection but may become increasingly difficult to sustain if the fighting continues and displaced families are unable to return home. The crisis has also created additional demands on local healthcare facilities, water supplies and emergency services. Humanitarian Response Faces Growing Challenges The rapid rise in displacement highlights the difficulty of responding to sudden population movements in areas where humanitarian resources are already limited. The Kenya Red Cross and local authorities are coordinating relief efforts, but additional assistance may be needed if more civilians cross the border. The emergency is unfolding against a wider backdrop of humanitarian funding challenges in the Horn of Africa. Earlier this month, the United Nations refugee agency warned that funding shortages had disrupted protection services for refugees in Ethiopia, including the suspension of registration affecting tens of thousands of Eritrean and South Sudanese refugees. That separate crisis illustrates broader pressure on humanitarian operations across the region, although it is not directly connected to the Moyale fighting. For families fleeing Moyale, the immediate concerns remain personal safety, temporary shelter, clean water, food and access to medical care. The latest displacement figures suggest that the consequences of the fighting have extended well beyond the original areas of confrontation. With the security situation still uncertain, humanitarian organizations face the challenge of assisting thousands of displaced people while preparing for the possibility of further arrivals. Whether the situation stabilizes will depend partly on efforts to end the violence and restore security in the affected border communities. Continue Reading Security Hundreds of Ethiopians Flee Into Kenya as Fighting Erupts in Moyale Hundreds flee Moyale fighting into Kenya as humanitarian concerns grow. Published 4 days ago on October 22, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Aerial view of a border-crossing complex, believed to be the Moyale One-Stop Border Post between Ethiopia and Kenya. File photo Source: Daily Nation (Nation Africa) Hundreds of Ethiopians, mostly women and children, have fled into neighboring Kenya following armed clashes in Moyale, a border town in southern Ethiopia’s Oromia region, according to Kenyan government officials and the Kenya Red Cross. The fighting began on Wednesday, October 21, 2026, and continued into Thursday, with residents reporting gunfire in parts of the town on the Ethiopian side of the border. The violence has forced families to seek safety across the international boundary, raising concerns about civilian protection and the humanitarian situation in the border area. According to a Reuters report , Marsabit County Commissioner Stanley Kamande confirmed on Thursday that hundreds of Ethiopian civilians had crossed into Kenya. Kamande said most of those arriving were women and children, many of whom were being accommodated by relatives and local residents in villages on the Kenyan side of Moyale. The exact number of displaced people has not yet been established. Kenya Red Cross Assesses Humanitarian Needs The Kenya Red Cross has begun assessing the scale of the displacement and the immediate needs of families who crossed the border. Ibrae John, an official with the humanitarian organization, told Reuters that no dedicated camp had been prepared to accommodate the displaced population. The organization was working to determine how many people had been affected and what assistance would be required. The absence of an established reception camp means that displaced families are currently relying heavily on relatives and host communities for shelter. It remains unclear how long the displaced residents will remain in Kenya or whether further arrivals are expected if the fighting continues. The latest movement of civilians highlights the humanitarian consequences of insecurity in Ethiopia’s southern borderlands, where communities maintain close social and economic ties across the international boundary. Cause of Moyale Fighting Remains Unclear The circumstances that triggered the latest fighting have not been independently established. Reuters reported that Ethiopian officials in the affected area could not immediately be reached for comment. The news agency did not identify the armed groups involved, and no independently verified casualty figures were available in its initial report. Residents said gunfire could still be heard on Thursday, indicating that the security situation had not fully stabilized. Moyale is divided by the Ethiopia-Kenya border, with communities on both sides maintaining close economic, cultural and family connections. Its location makes the town an important crossing point for trade, transportation and movement between the two countries. Armed violence in the area can therefore affect not only residents directly exposed to fighting but also traders, transport operators and families whose livelihoods depend on cross-border activity. The latest clashes come amid broader security challenges in Ethiopia, although no direct connection has been established between the Moyale violence and the renewed conflict in northern Tigray. Growing Concerns Over Civilian Displacement The arrival of hundreds of Ethiopians in Kenya adds to concerns about the humanitarian consequences of localized armed violence and the ability of border communities to accommodate displaced families. Women and children account for a substantial proportion of the people who have crossed into Kenya, according to the Marsabit County administration. Their immediate needs may include temporary shelter, food, clean water, healthcare and protection services, although the Kenya Red Cross assessment has not yet established the full requirements. The latest displacement also comes as humanitarian organizations across the Horn of Africa face significant funding pressures. Earlier this month, the United Nations refugee agency warned that severe financial shortages had disrupted protection services in Ethiopia. As detailed in Kulu Media’s report on refugee registration , UNHCR said funding constraints had forced the suspension of registration activities affecting tens of thousands of Eritrean and South Sudanese refugees in Ethiopia. That suspension concerns a separate refugee population and should not be confused with the latest movement of Ethiopians into Kenya. Nevertheless, the developments illustrate the wider pressure on humanitarian systems across the region. Border Security and Regional Implications The fighting in Moyale presents an immediate security and humanitarian challenge for authorities on both sides of the Ethiopia-Kenya border. Kenyan officials have confirmed the arrival of displaced civilians, but the Ethiopian authorities have not yet publicly explained the circumstances of the reported clashes. It also rema",
      "summary": "Renewed clashes erupted on October 26, 2026, near Baidoa, Somalia, between federal troops and fighters loyal to former South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen. The fighting occurred around Burjeedka, with reports of explosions and gunfire as military reinforcements were deployed. A Somali army commander claimed government forces inflicted casualties on their opponents, while a Laftagareen commander asserted his fighters forced federal troops to retreat. The conflict stems from ongoing disputes over regional control, exacerbated by recent territorial gains by Laftagareen's forces. This confrontation follows weeks of military preparations by both sides, with competing claims of success and an unclear casualty toll. The violence raises concerns for civilians in Baidoa, a humanitarian hub, as continued fighting threatens access to essential services and transportation routes. The situation reflects broader instability in South West State, where political disagreements have escalated into armed confrontations, complicating Somalia's military efforts against Al-Shabaab.",
